                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_04\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit's diff changes SecurityConfig.java from `@EnableWebSecurity` back to `@Configuration` (with `@Order(SecurityProperties.ACCESS_OVERRIDE_ORDER)`), fixing a \"MIME\" error. Tracing the exact same line across the candidate history:\n\n- candidate_01: Creates SecurityConfig.java from scratch with `@EnableWebSecurity`.\n- candidate_02: Changes the annotation to `@Configuration` (this was working correctly, alongside enabling CSRF token repository and other config changes).\n- candidate_03: Only touches an unrelated block (commenting out the AuthenticationManagerBuilder override); the annotation remains `@Configuration`.\n- candidate_04: Changes the annotation from `@Configuration` back to `@EnableWebSecurity` \u2014 this is the exact inverse of what the fix commit later reverts, and it is the last change to that line before the fix.\n- candidate_05: Is identical to the fix commit itself (reverting `@EnableWebSecurity` -> `@Configuration`), so it is not the bug-introducing commit but rather the fix.\n\nSince the fix diff exactly undoes the change made in candidate_04 (line-for-line: `@EnableWebSecurity` -> `@Configuration`), candidate_04 is the commit that directly introduced the regression that caused the MIME error. Using `@EnableWebSecurity` in combination with `@Order(SecurityProperties.ACCESS_OVERRIDE_ORDER)` on a `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter` causes Spring Boot's security auto-configuration to double-register filter chains, which is a known cause of static resources (JS/CSS) being served with incorrect/blocked MIME types.\n"
